# Gitignored paths that tracked files are allowed to reference.
#
# Every entry is a build artifact with a known generator. The rule this
# relaxes exists so a fresh clone builds; an entry here is a promise that the
# listed command runs before the compiler needs the file. If that command is
# not part of `just check`, the entry is a latent fresh-clone failure.
#
# Format: one repo-relative path per line. Comments explain the generator.
#
# Example (a Dioxus + Tailwind app, where `dx` compiles the stylesheet and
# `asset!()` then resolves it at compile time):
#
#   # generated by: dx build   (Dioxus.toml -> tailwind_output)
#   tmp/styles.css

# generated by: just dev   (app/Dioxus.toml -> tailwind_output)
#
# Read only by a browser, never by the compiler. That is the whole reason this
# entry is safe where the example above is not: `asset!()` resolves at compile
# time and would make a fresh clone fail to build, so the dashboard links this
# by URL instead and a build without it renders unstyled. See
# `docs/decisions/0025-the-stylesheet-is-fetched-not-linked-into-the-binary.md`.
app/assets/styles.css

# generated by: cargo build   (app/build.rs guarantees it, empty if need be)
#
# Read by the build script rather than by the compiler, and guaranteed to
# exist before anything looks — the same arrangement as the stylesheet above
# and for the same reason. Empty is the ordinary state: only `just build`
# puts anything in it, and only for the length of its second pass.
app/bundle/

# generated by: just installer   (from packaging/install.sh)
#
# The script a release publishes, templated from the tracked one with this
# release's version substituted in. Named by that recipe, by the release
# workflow that runs it, and by nothing that compiles — so unlike the entries
# above, no command has to run before the compiler wants it, because no
# compiler ever does. The rule being relaxed is that a fresh clone must build,
# and an artifact assembled in continuous integration is outside it entirely.
#
# The binaries themselves are not listed because they are not flagged: the
# check only considers tokens containing a dot, and `dist/stageman-linux-x64`
# has none. That is the heuristic's limit rather than a distinction worth
# relying on.
dist/install.sh
